{
  "title": "Shelf Split Squared Imbalance",
  "description": "## Task\n\nA row of items with given weights must be cut into exactly `groups` non-empty consecutive blocks. The cost of a block is the square of the sum of its weights. Find the smallest possible total cost over all ways to cut. Trying every cut quadratic in the length for every block count is too slow on the larger inputs.\n\n## Input\n\n- `weights` — a list of non-negative integers.\n- `groups` — the number of blocks, at most the length of `weights`.\n\n## Output\n\nThe minimum total of squared block sums.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nshelf_split_cost([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)  # returns 52\nshelf_split_cost([5, 5, 5], 3)  # returns 75\nshelf_split_cost([7, 1], 1)  # returns 64\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 ≤ length of `weights` ≤ 3000\n- 1 ≤ `groups` ≤ min(length, 60)\n- 0 ≤ each weight ≤ 1000\n",
  "signature": "def shelf_split_cost(weights: list[int], groups: int) -> int",
  "topic": "divide and conquer dp",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "category": "dynamic-programming",
